  "query_preview": "..\nToday and Tonight: Cooler air will continue into move into the\nregion today behind the strong dry cold front passage from\nSaturday that brought the extreme fire weather conditions to the\narea. Although weaker, winds will be locally breezy Sunday\nafternoon with gusts of 15-20 mph out of the west. Stronger gusts\nwill occur Sunday evening through the Cascade gaps with northwest\nwinds gusting up to 30 MPH. Winds will also be breezy out of the\nnorth for the Okanogan Valley Sunday Night into Monday, with winds\nine NE Washington shifting to northwest Sunday Night. These winds\nare in response to an upper trough dropping over the region Sunday\nNight out of the northwest. There is a cold pool aloft at 500mb\nnear -20C which will help steepen temperature lapse rates. Yet\nwith the dry low level air mass any cumulus buildups are unlikely\nto result in precipitation reaching the ground, other than\nsprinkles. A chance of sprinkles was added to the forecast however\nwith the increased instability.  \n\nMonday through Wednesday: After the trough exits, a dry northwest\nflow occurs over the region on Monday followed by high pressure\ngradually building into Tuesday and Wednesday. This will allow for\na warming trend with low relative humidity each day. Given the\nnorth-northwest flow on Monday more rounds of smoke will impact\nthe region from regional wildfires, including smoke coming down\nfrom British Columbia. Then with lighter winds and building high\npressure the smoke issues will likely continue. Several air\nquality agencies have issued Air Quality Alerts for several\ncounties across Central and Eastern Washington and north Idaho.\n\nThursday through Saturday: Ensemble means show high pressure\nbeginning to flatten late in the week, but with dry air\npersisting. The zonal flow and dry air mass will bring an uptick\nin wind speeds with ECMWF ensembles showing 15-25 MPH gusts \nacross Eastern Washington, and 25-35 MPH through the Cascade gaps\nby Friday. This has the potential to bring elevated to locally\ncritical fire weather conditions. JW",
